f0744ce672e68528d81c49bfa9" "q-104130/selection/5306f82049c45ad3a17e1ed88a928028b290b9edae55a6d3340e6f7ec897c56b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104130" :question/id #uuid "aae228c9-7097-5c88-acad-0bde9e65eba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104130" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{2}, & x<-2 \\\\ 4, & x=-2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=4,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)^{2}+4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{2} \\\\  & =4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-104130/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104130"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71079/selection/81c9afdf8ecc14ada0c171b8ffea5e3caac165b22cbf8045de7b5b1fa4380cb5" :answer/id #uuid "be7e0b08-5a90-57d5-816a-90a03a87c742" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71079/selection/61564c34803a5fd66926bbb1e37c7f7b4ad9bb9b307ffb4005ddc0ea753368ad" :answer/id #uuid "c93d2672-36dd-5a6e-a7be-bf009a506c37" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(-1)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71079/selection/1d1fa0cda59a3a735019a11434d7da00fd1e7776d202108cc4cf11f48759e91c" :answer/id #uuid "30718148-2db1-52d2-a576-e5ee103848aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71079/selection/6755a400c7f5d6354df14e56246b195bf31d0f44f2d3bd3db99e0fd0daf83a1b" :answer/id #uuid "84c6432b-bb7d-5d0a-a833-52e9e3ed110c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71079/selection/b5df0dfeb8df139972c5a9a30450abc8ef96aa263cd668d8bd50eb259a215728" :answer/id #uuid "52f619d7-987c-589b-b9ee-f7b59e15c5a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71079/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa1ee23b-914b-5080-960d-3273749cf149" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71079/selection/b5df0dfeb8df139972c5a9a30450abc8ef96aa263cd668d8bd50eb259a215728" :answer-field/choices ["q-71079/selection/81c9afdf8ecc14ada0c171b8ffea5e3caac165b22cbf8045de7b5b1fa4380cb5" "q-71079/selection/61564c34803a5fd66926bbb1e37c7f7b4ad9bb9b307ffb4005ddc0ea753368ad" "q-71079/selection/1d1fa0cda59a3a735019a11434d7da00fd1e7776d202108cc4cf11f48759e91c" "q-71079/selection/6755a400c7f5d6354df14e56246b195bf31d0f44f2d3bd3db99e0fd0daf83a1b" "q-71079/selection/b5df0dfeb8df139972c5a9a30450abc8ef96aa263cd668d8bd50eb259a215728"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71079" :question/id #uuid "6c5be8c7-1e7e-5512-992b-9c53ef828d54" :question/math-academy-id "q-71079" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}, & x<-1 \\\\ 2, & x=-1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- $f(-1)=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{2} \\\\  & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1,$ while $f(-1)=2.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-71079/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71079"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-48053"] :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $f(3)=0,$ so $f(3)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(2x+3) \\\\  & =2(3)+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9,$ while $f(3)=0.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-342262/field-1/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer/id #uuid "9cf659e0-7f4b-50b2-bebe-fbfa59ff5635" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342262/field-1/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer/id #uuid "f4fa1ece-0022-5b08-a869-39bb3931c8ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342262/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba3c26cd-5edd-5484-ad12-b972b769b637" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342262/field-1/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer-field/choices ["q-342262/field-1/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" "q-342262/field-1/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342262" :question/id #uuid "80d01b82-6f37-5dce-b739-67c5e497a5ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-342262" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7$ and $f(7)=5,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x)+1)^{2}}{x-f(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)=f(7)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x)+1)^{2}}{x-f(x)} & =\\frac{(5+1)^{2}}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342262/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342262"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104162/selection/3da001bfa72ea1d473a5791f1615c42f4e4226d910b244c49130063d46c63e38" :answer/id #uuid "71b699f2-7405-5059-a7ec-7b5c5c37942f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104162/selection/4f08b33bc1eefc8af8eac3e681f104578594f3c0520ed31f98f14b4a5118a02e" :answer/id #uuid "29cab8b8-72ad-5d84-a326-05c28d62cf90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104162/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "9f021756-67d6-5e55-9b02-c6128655c431" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104162/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer/id #uuid "3d3e13c8-f7e8-5392-a6c2-96992bb41259" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104162/selection/4d31c9cc5368f8cb8fa2d5661886836ed8f2cebf6125f1b80fa1f903bde9bebe" :answer/id #uuid "1360e0fe-8eaa-57c6-8106-b5133b25fd0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104162/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "17786bf9-1ca1-59ee-9cea-4709fc3100b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104162/selection/3da001bfa72ea1d473a5791f1615c42f4e4226d910b244c49130063d46c63e38" :answer-field/choices ["q-104162/selection/3da001bfa72ea1d473a5791f1615c42f4e4226d910b244c49130063d46c63e38" "q-104162/selection/4f08b33bc1eefc8af8eac3e681f104578594f3c0520ed31f98f14b4a5118a02e" "q-104162/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-104162/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" "q-104162/selection/4d31c9cc5368f8cb8fa2d5661886836ed8f2cebf6125f1b80fa1f903bde9bebe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104162" :question/id #uuid "bdf3ff45-feae-5253-9319-18b21bd915d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104162" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7$ and $f(7)=13,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {7}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-f(x))?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {7}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)=f(7)=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {7}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-f(x)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{2}-13) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-104162/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104162"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71070/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "ea4dc0f8-4ccf-5508-b344-cca9031370f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71070/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "6206ba08-ba2a-56c4-b4d2-8010ef75588f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71070/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "dc5a24d4-00d7-5d51-8581-9396a643b5d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71070/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "69eae88e-805e-5680-a836-f20ce88d2729" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71070/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "2e158cff-ec6e-57ee-93a0-a9eda22c8a98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71070/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "995acb94-f53b-5b6a-bb76-28f92f916fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71070/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-71070/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-71070/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-71070/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" "q-71070/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-71070/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71070" :question/id #uuid "c58af93f-518d-52f1-bdb2-b641e6313e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71070" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ and $f(0)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{({(f(x))}^{2}-3)}^{2}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=f(0)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{({(f(x))}^{2}-3)}^{2} & ={({2}^{2}-3)}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71070/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71070"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-38450"] :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}({(f(x))}^{2}+4) & =({2}^{2}+4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-37947/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "402b6471-23df-5086-8129-058acbdfa2ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37947/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "4b09ccb1-9233-5249-bb2d-0fb1eb0b92ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37947/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "42fb2d2a-029f-57bf-89dd-1b74e1bf8acf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37947/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "5c3e2839-1fc1-5a57-82a7-ed54147205dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37947/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "bdb24f55-d2fc-52ce-83cd-1fd764f90d8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37947/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c486000-eb22-5fa8-b3b3-9399b553d088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37947/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-37947/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-37947/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-37947/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-37947/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" "q-37947/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37947" :question/id #uuid "11906a0c-0ff1-503c-9c88-c67ebc9ab98c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37947" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4,$ then what is $f(\\frac{1}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{1}{2})={lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37947/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37947"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
